{"moat":{"rating":"narrow","sources_of_moat":["Brand and cultural equity (NIKE, Jordan). Nike is the largest athletic brand by revenue and held an estimated 27-30% of global athletic footwear in 2025","Scale in marketing, athlete and team endorsements, and supplier leverage. Endorsement commitments are about $15.5B, which smaller rivals cannot easily match","Global distribution across wholesale and owned/digital channels, with North America as the largest and most profitable sportswear market","Product innovation and R&D in performance categories. The performance business is about $16B and growing high-single digits"],"evidence":"<cite index=\"14-2\">Nike holds an estimated 27–30% share of global athletic footwear, versus about 11% for Adidas.</cite> The moat is eroding, though. <cite index=\"16-2,16-3\">Euromonitor puts Nike's 2025 global sports-footwear share at 22.9%, down from 25.9%, while Adidas rose from 11.7% to 12.2%.</cite> Consumer switching costs are negligible, so the moat depends on brand heat and execution. <cite index=\"12-5\">Switching costs for consumers are negligible for both Nike and Adidas.</cite> Evidence of damage: FY26 revenue was flat at $46.4B after a 10% fall in FY25, versus $51.4B in FY24. EBIT margin fell from 12.7% to 8.3%. Q1 FY27 was weak: <cite index=\"3-4\">Sportswear fell at a low-double-digit rate after Dunk sales were cut nearly 50%, Jordan fell at a mid-teens rate, and Greater China fell 26%.</cite> <cite index=\"15-1\">On grew 30%, New Balance 20%, Asics about 20%, Hoka 16% and Adidas 13%, while Nike shrank 10% in its most recent full year.</cite> Pricing power is dented but not gone: gross margin is still about 42.8% and is recovering. Nike's absolute scale means challengers could take share for years before threatening it. Rating is narrow rather than wide because the brand has been impaired, share has been lost, and the recovery is unproven. Whether the damage is cyclical or structural is unknown."},"management":{"rating":"average","capital_allocation":"The record is mixed. Historically Nike was a heavy buyer back: about 124.4M shares at an average of $97.57, roughly $12.1B, with about $5.9B of authorization left. Buybacks have been paused since Q1 FY26. The current price of $34.61 is well below that average, so past repurchases destroyed value in hindsight. FY26 dividends of $2,407M exceeded free cash flow of $2,184M, so the dividend is not covered by FCF while earnings are depressed. Debt is about $7.9B plus leases, against cash of $8.4B. Capex rose to $684M from $430M. FY26 EPS was $2.10, but FY27 adjusted EPS is guided to $1.15-$1.35, so the payout ratio is under strain. The new 'Pace' program targets about $2.5B of savings for about $1.0B of charges through FY31. <cite index=\"3-6\">FY27 revenue is expected to fall at a high-single-digit rate, with EBIT falling by an even larger percentage.</cite> <cite index=\"2-8\">The CFO warned that actions in Sportswear, Jordan and China will dampen results through this year and likely into fiscal 2028.</cite> Positives: <cite index=\"1-2,1-3\">Q1 gross margin rose 60 bps to 42.8% and SG&A fell 3%.</cite> Q1 EPS of $0.48 beat the $0.43 consensus. CEO Elliott Hill is a 38-year Nike insider (joined 1988) and has run the company since October 2024. The turnaround is taking longer than first hoped: <cite index=\"27-15\">Nike says the changes could take 24 to 36 months to show fully in results.</cite> The FY26 gross margin was also helped by a one-off $986M IEEPA tariff recovery credit. Overall, execution is improving in performance categories but financial results remain poor, hence 'average'.","governance_concerns":["Dual-class structure: Class A (about 281M shares) and Class B (about 1.2B shares). Co-founder Phil Knight holds about 17.7% per Simply Wall St. The exact voting-power split and board-election rights are not in the retrieved material, so treat them as unknown beyond the existence of separate Class A and Class B director slates","Executive Committee is Mark Parker (Executive Chairman, former CEO 2006-2020), Travis Knight and Elliott Hill. Parker remains deeply involved after a long tenure, which raises questions about board independence and accountability for the 2020-2024 strategy missteps","Management turnover: new CFO Dave Denton (ex-Pfizer, joined August 2026) replaced Matthew Friend. <cite index=\"28-4\">Average management team tenure is 1.8 years.</cite> The Chief Operating Officer role was only created in December 2025. A new senior hire, Alexandre Arnault, was announced in September 2026","Insider-heavy turnaround: <cite index=\"27-20\">Fortune says institutional knowledge needs to be paired with strong outside perspectives, particularly on branding and culture.</cite>","Pay: Hill's reported total compensation is about $36.3M (Simply Wall St) while earnings are down sharply, and his direct share ownership is minimal at about 0.005%. Pay-for-performance alignment is unknown","Lender/shareholder-return optics: the dividend exceeds FCF and buybacks were paused after about $12B was spent at an average price near $98","Earnings quality: FY26 results include a $986M tariff-recovery benefit in cost of sales and $385M of severance. Restructuring charges are recurring (about $1.0B more under Pace)"]},"industry":{"growth_outlook":"The long-term global athletic footwear and apparel market grows at a mid-single-digit rate, driven by fitness, running and athleisure. One source cites about 7.2% growth in 2025, which is a low-quality source. Growth is concentrated in running and performance specialists and in resurgent Adidas, not in Nike. Nike's own FY27 revenue is guided down high-single digits. Greater China is weak: <cite index=\"2-6\">the CFO's guidance assumes China revenue gets worse for the rest of the year.</cite> Tariffs are a headwind: the 10-K shows a net IEEPA recovery, but a source cites about a $1.5B FY2026 impact. A rebound is expected in North America performance. <cite index=\"7-9\">North America revenue grew 2% in Q1.</cite> Industry growth is solid, but Nike's ability to participate in it is unproven.","competitive_intensity":"High and rising. Share is shifting away from Nike toward running specialists and a rebuilt Adidas. <cite index=\"15-5\">The competitive map now includes a resurgent Adidas, two premium running insurgents (On and Hoka), a quietly enormous New Balance, and a comeback-mode Asics.</cite> <cite index=\"14-7\">On and Hoka have grabbed mindshare in performance running, while Adidas has won back style-led consumers through Samba and Gazelle.</cite> Local rivals such as Anta are strong in China. Wholesale partners had been given shelf space to rivals during Nike's wholesale pullback, and Nike is now rebuilding those relationships. Nike's discounting has risen.","key_competitors":["Adidas","On Holding","Hoka (Deckers)","New Balance","Asics","Puma","Under Armour","Skechers","Lululemon","Anta Sports (China)"]},"catalysts":["Performance categories (running, football, basketball, training; about $16B in FY26) continue growing high-single digits and begin to outweigh Sportswear declines. Running has grown for six straight quarters","Stabilisation of Sportswear/Dunk and Jordan after deliberate supply cuts, with cleaner full-price channels","Greater China reset (digital marketplace clean-up, premium repositioning) bottoming. Management expects it to worsen before improving","Pace program: about $2.5B of cumulative savings through FY31, a supply chain shift to a more flexible structure, a new Bengaluru campus, and a three-geography reorganisation (Americas, APGC, EMEA) from FY28","Gross margin recovery (Q1 +60 bps to 42.8%) as tariffs, logistics and discounting normalise","Wholesale rebuilding and 'Family footwear' in the $65-$85 price segment","New CFO Dave Denton may bring capital allocation discipline. Dividend and buyback policy are open questions","Key negative checkpoints: Q2 FY27 results and FY28 guidance. Management said pressure may extend into FY28, so the timing of a profit trough is unknown","Event-driven demand from the 2026 World Cup (football benefited in Q1) and college/pro sports partnerships"],"citations":["https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-050050135.html","https://www.fool.com/earnings/call-transcripts/2026/10/02/nike-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-transcript/","https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/stocks/articles/nike-q1-earnings-call-highlights-000223968.html","https://ca.investing.com/news/company-news/nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-highlights-performance-gains-offset-by-china-reset-and--4863264","https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/01/nike-nke-q1-2027-earnings.html","https://singjupost.com/transcript-nike-inc-nke-q1-2027-earnings-call-conference/","https://www.companieshistory.com/nike-swot-analysis/","https://founderpin.com/nike-vs-adidas-2026-which-sportswear-brand-leads/","https://businessmodelanalyst.com/nike-competitors-alternatives/","https://koalagains.com/stocks/NYSE/NKE/competition","https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000320187/000032018726000070/pressrelease062326.htm","https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000320187/000032018726000089/nikecourtesypdf2026.pdf","https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000320187/000032018726000088/nke-20260531.htm","https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0000320187/000032018726000170/a091626pressrelease.htm","https://fortune.com/article/nike-ceo-veteran-elliott-hill-turnaround-growth/","https://simplywall.st/stocks/de/consumer-durables/etr-nke/nike-shares/management"]}
