 "bear_case": [
  "The base FCF of $4.023B is the average of FY24-FY26 (6.617+3.268+2.184)/3. It includes the FY24 peak and is 84% above FY26 FCF of $2.184B. Q1 FY27 FCF was about -$64M. Valuation scales linearly with the FCF input. Rescaled to FY26 FCF, my approximate values are: base about $22-23 (-35%), bear about $13, and bull about $33, which is still below the $34.61 price. The headline +25% base upside comes from the starting-FCF choice, not from operations.",
  "FY27 guidance is for revenue down high-single digits and EBIT down by more than that. Adjusted EPS is guided to $1.15-1.35 against $2.10 in FY26, a 36-45% drop. At $34.61 that is about 26-30x guided EPS. The 6% growth applied from year one therefore starts in a year when management guides to a sharp decline.",
  "FY26 earnings are flattered by the $986M IEEPA credit in cost of sales. Excluding it, operating income is about $2.8B and gross margin about 40.8%, versus 42.7% in FY25. That is roughly 190 bps of underlying gross margin erosion, and flat operating income only held up because of the credit. The credit is non-repeatable, and $684M was still a receivable at year-end. The cost incurred is not quantified, so the true net effect is uncertain.",
  "Dividends of $2.407B exceed FCF of $2.184B. In Q1 FY27 dividends were about $610M against FCF of about -$64M. Deducting SBC ($715M), FCF is only about $1.47B, a yield of about 2.9% against a dividend yield of about 4.7%. $2.0B of debt is current. Cash is $8.4B, but it is burning about $0.7B a quarter before the roughly $0.3B of FY27 Pace cash charges. A dividend cut or more leverage is plausible, even though the A+/A2 ratings hold for now.",
  "Share loss looks structural. Euromonitor puts Nike's share at 22.9%, down from 25.9%. On, New Balance, Asics, Hoka and Adidas are growing at 13-30%, and consumer switching costs are negligible. Q1 FY27 showed Sportswear down low double digits, Jordan down mid-teens, Greater China down 26% with EBIT down 34%, and Converse down 28%. Management says the pressure runs into FY28 and that the turnaround takes 24-36 months. The moat is narrow and eroding, so a 6% CAGR from a trough is not a safe base case.",
  "Earnings and cash-flow quality are weak. Receivables rose 25.7% on 0.2% revenue growth. Sales and returns reserves fell (-$245M and -$178M) while wholesale returns and discounts are rising. The inventory reserve fell to $213M while Converse cites higher obsolescence. About 30% of accounts payable ($1.1B) is supplier finance. Hedge gains collapsed from $295M to $20M, and are now a loss. Restructuring charges keep recurring: $443M in FY24, $385M in FY26, and about $1.0B more under Pace. These items point to margin and working-capital risk ahead, not just timing.",
  "Tail risks are unquantified: the Belgian customs claim (no loss range, $1.3B of bank guarantees), $953M of unrecognized tax benefits plus $438M of interest and penalties, an IRS audit of FY17-23, the EU State Aid case, and securities class actions. The CFO left right after signing the 10-K. The COO role is new, average executive tenure is 1.8 years, and the control structure (Swoosh LLC, Class A elects three-quarters of the board) limits shareholder recourse.",
  "Fixed commitments of about $15.5B in endorsements, $7.3B in purchase obligations and $3.1B in leases create operating leverage on the downside. Revenue is falling, so these costs cannot flex."
 ],
 "assumption_challenges": [
  {
   "assumption": "Base FCF of $4.023B (3-year average) as the starting point",
   "why_questionable": "It is not documented in the analysis, which shows FY26 FCF of $2.184B and a Q1 FY27 FCF of about -$64M. The average includes FY24 ($6.6B) before the revenue decline, the margin compression and the working-capital swing. Using it assumes an immediate 84% FCF rebound, which contradicts the FY27 guidance. The 'bear' scenario also uses the same inflated base, so it is not a real bear case."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "6% FCF growth for five years (low-confidence judgment)",
   "why_questionable": "Management guides FY27 revenue down high-single digits and EBIT down more, and says pressure likely extends into FY28. Pace savings of $2.5B are cumulative over five years, before reinvestment, and come with about $1.0B of charges. That is about $0.5B a year gross, and the savings may be reinvested. The growth rationale cites working-capital reversal, but Q1 FY27 showed no reversal. Growth should start from a lower FY27 level and then recover, not compound from $4.0B."
