  {
   "assumption": "Discount rates of 9-12% and terminal growth of 2-3.5%",
   "why_questionable": "For a business losing share, with negligible switching costs and unproven recovery, a 3-3.5% perpetual growth rate and a 9-10% discount rate are generous. Terminal value is the majority of EV, so these inputs dominate the result. The scenario spread is also artificially narrow on the downside because the base FCF is the same in every case."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "FY26 operating income and gross margin are a reasonable run-rate",
   "why_questionable": "They include a $986M one-time tariff credit, partly offset by $385M of severance. The ex-credit gross margin is about 40.8%. Q1 FY27's +60 bps comparison and the 'gross margin recovering' narrative rely on a base that includes hedge, FX and tariff noise. EV/EBITDA of 11x and a trailing P/E of 16x are flattered by the credit. Ex-credit FY26 EPS is about $1.55-1.60, which is about 22x."
  },
  {
   "assumption": "Forward P/E of 20.3x as a valuation anchor",
   "why_questionable": "20.3x implies forward EPS of about $1.70, but guided FY27 adjusted EPS is $1.15-1.35. The source of the 20.3x is not documented. On guidance, the stock is not cheap on forward earnings."
