   "why_questionable": "Reported debt in market data includes leases, and a DCF that nets lease debt while using FCF after lease expense double-counts a little. More important, $2.0B matures within a year, cash is falling with negative Q1 FCF, and endorsement obligations ($15.5B) are not counted as debt-like commitments."
  }
 ],
 "inconsistencies_found": [
  "The valuation uses a base FCF of $4.023B, but the filings analysis headline FCF is $2.184B, and its FCF yield (about 4.2%) uses $2.184B. The DCF therefore implies an FCF yield on the same market cap of about 7.8% in year one, which is incompatible with the rest of the analysis.",
  "The growth rationale describes a 'trough base', but the 3-year-average base is not a trough. The trough is FY26 or Q1 FY27 (negative FCF). The 6% rate is therefore applied to a base that is not the trough.",
  "The forward P/E of 20.3x (implied EPS about $1.70) conflicts with the qualitative section's FY27 adjusted EPS guidance of $1.15-1.35 (implied 26-30x). One of them is wrong or uses different EPS definitions.",
  "The qualitative analysis cites Nike's global athletic footwear share as 27-30% (a low-quality source) and also 22.9% (Euromonitor, down from 25.9%). The moat evidence uses both without reconciling them. The higher figure supports the 'narrow moat' rating, but the Euromonitor decline of 3.0 points is the more relevant trend.",
  "The filings analysis says gross margin 'recovering' (+60 bps in Q1), but FY26 gross margin of 42.9% includes the $986M credit. Ex-credit it is about 40.8%, below FY25's 42.7%. The 40.8% figure also ignores the tariff costs the credit offset, so the underlying drag is not cleanly measured.",
  "The growth rationale cites working-capital reversal (including the IEEPA receivable collected after year-end) as a tailwind, but Q1 FY27 OCF was only $135M, so the collection did not produce a recovery. The rationale acknowledges this but still adds the argument to the 6% case.",
  "Market data's total debt ($11,125M) differs from balance sheet debt plus leases ($11,072M), and FY23 figures cannot be reconciled to the filings. Minor, but it shows the DCF inputs are not fully tied to the filings.",
  "The qualitative section's 'positives' (EPS beat of $0.48 vs $0.43, SG&A -3%) coexist with net income down 2% and Q1 revenue down 4-5%. The beat was against a lowered consensus, and the effective tax rate was 22.7%, so the EPS beat does not show underlying improvement.",
  "The valuation flag 'reliable: true' with no notes does not mention that the base FCF is 84% above the latest year and 'bear' still uses it, so the reliability flag overstates confidence."
 ],
 "severity": "minor",
 "severity_reason": "The flaws are mostly in valuation and earnings quality, not a franchise-ending issue. The brand, scale, A+/A2 ratings and $8.4B of cash mean solvency or fraud are not the concern. The severe problem is in the thesis itself: normalizing the DCF to FY26 FCF removes the claimed 25% upside (base about $22-23). The FY27 EPS guide is down about 40%, and the dividend exceeds FCF. That makes the stock unattractive at $34.61 and the base case unreliable, but it is not a reason to avoid the stock at any price."
