{"bear_case":["The base FCF input of $4.023B is not current FCF. It equals the 3-year average of FY24-FY26 FCF: (6.617+3.268+2.184)/3 = 4.023. That is 84% above FY26 FCF of $2.184B and includes the FY24 peak of $6.6B. FY26 FCF was also down 33% year over year, and Q1 FY27 FCF was about -$64M. Rescaling the base DCF to $2.184B gives roughly $22-23 per share, about 35% below the $34.61 price. The +25% base-case upside therefore comes from the input choice, not from the business.","Even the 'bear' case ($25.77) starts from the inflated $4.0B base, so it is not a real downside case. A real bear case has FCF of $1-2B, a dividend cut and a lower multiple. That points to roughly $15-22 per share.","FY26 earnings are flattered by a one-time $986M IEEPA tariff recovery. Operating income was $3,797M, up 2.6% from $3,702M. Excluding the recovery it was about $2.8B, down roughly 25%. Gross margin ex-recovery is about 40.8%, versus 42.7% in FY25. Net EPS ex-recovery is about $1.55-1.60 (at a ~20% tax rate), not $2.10. The trailing P/E of 16.3x is therefore closer to 22x on clean earnings. EV/EBITDA of 11.1x is about 14x ex-recovery.","Guidance implies the P/E is not cheap. FY27 adjusted EPS is guided to $1.15-1.35, so the price is about 26-30x guided EPS. Q1 EPS was $0.48, which leaves only about $0.67-0.87 for Q2-Q4, or roughly $0.22-0.29 per quarter. That points to a sharp deterioration in the back half. Revenue is guided down at a high-single-digit rate and EBIT down by more, so the profit trough is still ahead.","The dividend is not covered. FY26 dividends were $2,407M against FCF of $2,184M. In Q1 FY27 dividends were $610M against OCF of $135M. Against the $1.15-1.35 EPS guide, the payout is roughly 120-140%. Buybacks are already paused, so a dividend cut or balance-sheet funding is the likely next step. A cut would hit the income-oriented holder base. Funding it from cash would run down the $8.4B cash pile, of which $2.0B of debt is current.","Cash conversion is deteriorating even before the restructuring cash costs arrive. About $684M of IEEPA receivable was collected after year-end, yet Q1 FY27 OCF was only $135M. If that collection landed in Q1, underlying Q1 OCF was about -$550M. The 'working capital timing' argument for FCF recovery is therefore weak. Receivables are +25.7% against revenue +0.2%. Sales and returns reserves fell while management says returns and discounts are rising. About 30% of accounts payable is supplier-finance-linked.","Operating leverage is working in reverse. About $15.5B of endorsement obligations, $4.9B of product purchase commitments and about $3.1B of leases are largely fixed. Revenue is still falling (Q1 -4%, FY27 guided down at a high-single-digit rate), so margins have more room to fall.","Competitive share loss looks structural, not cyclical. Euromonitor puts Nike's global sports-footwear share at 22.9%, down from 25.9%. On, New Balance, Asics, Hoka and Adidas are growing at 13-30% while Nike shrank. Consumer switching costs are negligible. Sportswear is down at a low-double-digit rate, Jordan is down at a mid-teens rate, and Greater China is down 26% (EBIT -34%) with management guiding it worse. This is a brand-heat problem, and cost cuts do not fix it.","The Pace savings are overstated as a driver of FCF. The $2.5B is cumulative through FY31, before reinvestment and before about $1.0B of charges. The annual run-rate is small relative to the revenue and margin decline, and savings are likely back-loaded. Prior programs (FY24 $443M, FY26 $385M of charges) have not restored profitability. Leadership has also stated that pressure likely extends into FY28.","Tariff and sourcing risk remains. Vietnam makes 52% of footwear, Indonesia 27% and China 16%, and four contract manufacturers make about 60% of footwear. The IEEPA recovery is a one-off. Replacement tariff regimes are not quantified, and the hedge gains that cushioned cost of sales have collapsed ($295M to $20M to a $30M loss).","Tail liabilities are unquantified. The Belgian customs claim has no loss range or accrual, and bank guarantees are up to $1.3B from $0.9B. Unrecognized tax benefits are $953M plus $438M of interest and penalties. An IRS audit of FY2017-2023, the EC State Aid case and securities class actions are also open. Together these could absorb a meaningful share of a $2B FCF year.","Governance and execution continuity are weak. Swoosh LLC controls about 79% of Class A shares, which elect three-quarters of the board, so minority holders have little leverage. The CFO left within about a month of signing the 10-K. The COO role is new, average management tenure is 1.8 years, and the Executive Chairman is the former CEO who oversaw the 2020-2024 strategy.","Past capital allocation destroyed value: $12.1B of buybacks at an average of $97.57 against a $34.61 price. The 76.8M options at an average strike of $96.44 are deep out of the money, so SBC of $715M (about 33% of FY26 FCF) is effectively replaced by cash grants or dilution."],"assumption_challenges":[{"assumption":"Base FCF of $4.023B is the starting point for the DCF","why_questionable":"This is the 3-year average including the FY24 peak ($6.6B). FY26 FCF was $2.184B, Q1 FY27 was about -$64M, and FY27 EBIT is guided to fall faster than revenue. FY25 FCF was also flattered by capex of only $430M, versus $684M in FY26 and $812-969M earlier. The base is not normalised; it assumes a recovery that has not happened."},{"assumption":"6% growth for five years from the base","why_questionable":"Applied to $4.0B, this puts year-5 FCF at about $5.4B, which is about 2.5x FY26 FCF and about 20% a year compounded from the actual FY26 level. The filings analysis itself calls the 6% 'low confidence' and says it sits between a recovery and a stagnation case. The rationale mixes a trough base with a mid-cycle growth rate, which double-counts the recovery."},{"assumption":"FY26 OCF was depressed by working capital that will reverse","why_questionable":"About $684M of the receivable was collected after year-end, but Q1 FY27 OCF was only $135M. The rationale itself notes offsets from compensation and tax payments. FY27 will also carry about $0.3B of cash restructuring charges, higher capex and Greater China declines. Any reversal is already visible and is not delivering."},{"assumption":"Gross margin is recovering (+60 bps in Q1 to 42.8%)","why_questionable":"FY26 gross margin of 42.9% includes a $986M one-off, or about 210 bps, so the underlying figure is about 40.8%. FX was +40 bps of the +60 bps in Q1, leaving about +20 bps of underlying improvement. Hedge gains have turned into losses. Management says the tariff recovery 'largely offset' FY26 tariff costs, so the true underlying margin is ambiguous, and nothing shows a clean recovery."},{"assumption":"10% discount rate and 3% terminal growth in the base case","why_questionable":"This is a share-losing, controlled, dual-class company with leadership turnover and legal and tax overhangs. Terminal growth of 3% for a brand losing share to faster-growing rivals is generous. The bull case uses a 9% discount rate and 3.5% terminal growth, which assumes a full brand recovery."},{"assumption":"Moat rated 'narrow' and management 'average'","why_questionable":"The qualitative section cites a 27-30% share (a low-quality source) alongside Euromonitor's 22.9%, down 300 bps in a year. It says the moat is eroding and switching costs are negligible, which fits 'none or fragile' better than 'narrow'. Management has a 1.8-year average tenure, a CFO exit, extended turnaround timelines, a dividend above FCF and a $12B buyback at about $98, which supports 'below average' on capital allocation."},{"assumption":"Performance categories (about $16B, +HSD) will outweigh Sportswear declines","why_questionable":"Total revenue was flat in FY26 and down 4% in Q1 FY27, so the growth in performance is not outrunning declines elsewhere. Management guides revenue down at a high-single-digit rate for FY27. Nothing in the material shows when the crossover occurs."},{"assumption":"Forward P/E of 20.3x is a usable reference","why_questionable":"The source of the forward EPS is not given, and no analyst estimates are available. A 20.3x forward P/E implies about $1.70 EPS, well above the $1.15-1.35 guidance. On guidance the forward P/E is about 26-30x."}],"inconsistencies_found":["The valuation is flagged 'reliable: true' with no notes, yet the base FCF ($4.023B) is the 3-year average rather than latest or normalised FCF. The analysis never discloses this, and it conflicts with its own FCF history showing a sharp downtrend.","The growth rationale says FCF is at a 'depressed base', yet the DCF starts at an FCF 84% above FY26. It treats the trough as depressed but uses a base that already includes partial recovery.","The filings analysis flags the $986M tariff recovery as inflating FY26 gross margin (about 40.8% ex-recovery), while the qualitative section describes margins as 'recovering' and says pricing power is 'dented but not gone', with 42.8% as the baseline.","The filings analysis says the $684M receivable was largely collected after year-end, a favourable working-capital point. Q1 FY27 OCF was still only $135M, which implies underlying weakness that the growth rationale softens by attributing the shortfall to 'other offsets'.","The qualitative section says the exact voting split and board rights are unknown and cites Phil Knight at about 17.7%. The filings analysis states that Swoosh LLC owns about 79% of Class A and that Class A holders elect three-quarters of the board. The control picture is incomplete in the qualitative section.","Market-share figures conflict: 27-30% for Nike versus 11% for Adidas in one source, and 22.9% versus 12.2% in Euromonitor. The qualitative section uses both without reconciling the 5-7 point gap.","The qualitative section cites 'CEO Elliott Hill ... 38-year Nike insider' as a positive and also quotes Fortune saying the turnaround needs outside perspective. It also rates management 'average' while listing a dividend above FCF, a $12B buyback at about $98 versus a $34.61 price, and leadership churn.","FY27 EPS guidance of $1.15-1.35 implies roughly 40% lower EPS than FY26's $2.10. Q1 EPS ($0.48) is down only 2% year over year. The 'Q1 EPS beat consensus' framing ignores that this implies a much weaker remaining three quarters.","Total debt differs between sources: Market Data shows $11,125M and the balance sheet plus leases shows $11,072M. This is minor, but the cash figure of $8,368M also includes short-term investments with current debt of $2.0B due, so net cash is not truly net.","Capex of $430M in FY25 (versus $969M in FY23 and $812M in FY24) is used in the FCF history without noting it as abnormally low. FY25 and the 3-year average FCF are flattered by this underinvestment.","The data gaps list says no analyst estimates exist, yet the multiples include a forward P/E and the qualitative section cites a $0.43 consensus EPS. The analysis relies on unsourced forward data while claiming none exists."],"severity":"major","severity_reason":"The upside rests on a base FCF input that is 84% above current FCF and that Q1 FY27 and FY27 guidance contradict. On FY26 FCF the base DCF gives about $22-23 per share, below the price. FY26 profit includes a $986M one-off, the dividend exceeds FCF, FY27 EPS is guided about 40% lower, and underlying Q1 operating cash flow looks negative even after a large receivable collection. This is driven by cash generation and earnings trajectory, not by fraud or insolvency: the A+/A2 credit rating and $8.4B of cash limit solvency risk. Even so, the buy thesis as stated is not supported."}
